# No Silent Deferral

**Every piece of work that surfaces mid-task ends in exactly one of two states: done with proof, or explicitly surfaced with an approval ask.** "Slotted for the future" without asking is the failure this rule kills (instituted by Phi 2026-08-29 after a session parked agent-doable items as "queued" without asking).

## The contract

- Before claiming anything complete, run an executable check (test, build, lint, probe) and show its output. Never assert success without it. Transport acknowledgments (message queued, dispatch accepted, `success:true`) are not evidence of completion ([[no-unverified-claims]]).
- Never present TODO, stub, or placeholder markers as finished work. If something must be deferred, say so in the same message, never buried in a comment.
- When a requirement is blocked or needs authority you lack (credentials, sudo, a permission denial, a paid resource), keep it open with a named blocker and ask. Never silently substitute a narrower goal and report done.
- Newly discovered subtasks: if in-scope and safe, do them. Otherwise add a "NEEDS APPROVAL: {item}" line to the next user-facing message. Surfacing is mandatory either way. No third "later" bucket.
- Do not expand scope beyond what was approved without flagging it. Silent widening is as much a deviation as silent narrowing.
- Mark a scope decision you made unilaterally as your own assumption. Later turns must never cite it as user-approved intent.
- Ambiguous instructions: ask before implementing. A guess that collapses the task is worse than one question.

Worker prompts inherit this via the discipline block's ABSTAIN / ANTI-DEFERRAL section (references/discipline-block.md). Subagents never see this rule directly, so the block is the only copy they get.

Evidence base (the "trust-then-verify gap", the /goal pattern, requirement omission as the #1 agent failure at 27.9%, cline #9154): the-relevant-entry.
